gearbench (1.2.0) resolute; urgency=medium

  * New keyboards: the G915 X LIGHTSPEED, which has run on the hardware on
    its cable and through its receiver, and trials not yet checked there:
    the G915 X, the G915 X LIGHTSPEED TKL, the G316 X and G316 X 98, the
    G512 X 75 and G512 X 98, the PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED and the G213 Prodigy.
    Each but the G213 Prodigy gets per-key lighting, the animations it
    reports and brightness, and the LIGHTSPEED models their battery; the
    G213 Prodigy is lit in five areas, a color each, with its animations
    and no brightness of its own. The G915 X LIGHTSPEED's G1 to G9 and the
    PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED's F1 to F12 bind to a key or a macro; the G915 X's
    G1 to G9, and F1 to F12 on the G915 X LIGHTSPEED TKL, the G316 X pair
    and the G512 X pair, bind once tested on each model. The G512 X's
    analog keys are not set yet.
  * On the G515 TKL, the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL and the G915 X, G316 X and
    G512 X models, FN and the keys it gives a second function turn white
    while FN is held. For that, Gearbench asks the keyboard for FN at each
    setup, and until the keyboard loses power its control connection,
    which the udev rules let any program you run open, carries each press
    and release of Ctrl, Shift, Alt, Super and FN; Gearbench reads FN
    alone. Pulsar point, a two-color animation of the keyboard's own, runs
    on the models that list it, and Remind me of, Neon and Outer space,
    not yet run on a keyboard, on the G512 X 75 and G512 X 98.
  * New mice, trials not yet checked on the hardware: the
    PRO X SUPERLIGHT 2 (the SUPERLIGHT 2 SE too), the PRO X SUPERLIGHT 2
    DEX and 2c, the PRO X2 SUPERSTRIKE and the G203 LIGHTSYNC (the G102
    LIGHTSYNC too). Gearbench reads each and sets its DPI and report rate,
    the PRO X ones' in host mode alone, and shows their battery; in host
    mode it drives the buttons, and it sets the G203's lighting. Nothing is
    written to their onboard memory. The PRO X2 SUPERSTRIKE's actuation
    point, rapid trigger and haptics are not set yet.
  * A mouse with a battery posts low-battery notices below 30% and again
    below 15%, as the keyboards and headsets do. Each gearbench-ctl command
    for one mouse takes --mouse, a model's name or one of its product ids.
  * New headsets: the Astro A50 X, which has run on the hardware. Gearbench
    sets its sidetone, volume, Game and Voice balance, noise gate, auto
    sleep, tones and stream mix, its base station's deep sleep and light
    brightness, and its two equalizers, the headphones' and the
    microphone's, each with the three presets the headset holds and
    presets of your own kept on this computer. GNOME lists its Game and
    Chat as two outputs. Read only until tested: the Astro A50 Gen 5, the
    PRO X 2 LIGHTSPEED, the G522 LIGHTSPEED and the G325 LIGHTSPEED, whose
    battery, sidetone, auto sleep and equalizer Gearbench shows.
  * Ten more headsets of the G432's family: the G933, which has run on the
    hardware, and trials not yet checked there: the G431, G433, G533,
    G535, G633, G635, G733, G935 and PRO X Wireless. Each gets its
    sidetone and microphone noise suppression, and the wireless ones their
    battery and low-battery notices. The G933, G935 and G633 get the
    equalizer in the headset, with presets kept on this computer, and the
    others the G432's ten-band equalizer on the computer. The G733, G933,
    G935, G633 and G635 get their lights, and the G933, G935, G633 and
    G635 their G1 to G3. On the G933 Gearbench also writes the auto
    shut-off, the lights' startup effect and the lighting the headset
    shows on its own, and refuses those writes on a model not yet tested.
  * Gearbench Spatial plays to every new headset, picked in the window or
    with headset-spatial --headset. Each headset page shows its
    microphone's volume as GNOME's Sound settings set it. Not yet tested on
    a headset: Input level, a live meter of the microphone on each headset
    page, to set a noise gate by, and a headset's lights following the
    keyboard that leads, as a mouse's do.
  * Every keyboard plugged in is driven at once, where one was driven at a
    time. One leads: the others show its lighting at its brightness unless
    set to keep their own, and the mice follow it. The keyboards dim
    together, and an M key on any of them switches every keyboard's M
    profile. The switcher at the top of the Keyboard tab now picks the
    keyboard the tab shows, and the Lighting group's Leader row the one
    that leads. The drawn keyboard there shows the animation the keyboard
    runs, a close likeness of it.
  * A game profile can give a mouse that follows the keyboard a color of
    its own, in the Game profiles dialog or with gearbench-ctl
    profile-mouse-color, and each of the pack's 67 game profiles gives
    one. Importing the pack again adds it to a pack profile imported
    before whose lighting is still the pack's.
  * Each mouse's pointer moves at the DPI Gearbench sets. The package gives
    each supported mouse a DPI of 1000 in the system's hardware database;
    the desktop scaled down a mouse that database gives a higher default,
    so a G502 HERO set to 1800 DPI moved like an unlisted mouse at 750. A
    mouse plugged in during the install takes the new speed at its next
    plug-in.
  * Gearbench sets up a keyboard, or a mouse's cable, as soon as it is
    plugged in, where it looked every 2 seconds. A keyboard switched off
    behind its receiver no longer holds up the other keyboard's keys and
    the mice every 2 seconds.
  * Gearbench 1.1.0 refuses a config.json or game profile that binds a key
    under a name new in this version, such as G515-F1, G915X-G1 or
    G933-G1, and writes nothing over it. To go back to 1.1.0, clear those
    keys first, as the guide's Settings files section says.
  * Among the fixes: a G515 TKL keeps the brightness its FN+F8 sets, where
    Gearbench wrote its own saved level over it, and a keyboard idle on
    its receiver shows a lighting change within about 2 seconds, where the
    change could wait 30 seconds or until a key press. Security fixes: the
    daemon's service makes a missing runtime folder itself before each
    start, where another account could make it first below a folder others
    may write in; each folder Gearbench makes in the settings folder is
    yours alone, whatever the login umask; gearbench-ctl run under sudo
    talks only to the daemon of the user who ran it; and the About dialog's
    Debugging Information page writes your home folder as ~, so a report
    pasted into an issue no longer gives your user name.

 -- wmuschetto <113629853+wmuschetto@users.noreply.github.com>  Wed, 28 Oct 2026 03:40:00 +0000

